<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Calderón de la Barca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1600">1600</a>–<a href="/years/1681">1681</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Calderón de la Barca was a Spanish dramatist and poet, the leading playwright of the later Golden Age. His plays include Life Is a Dream, a philosophical drama on free will and illusion, and The Mayor of Zalamea, as well as many sacramental plays performed for the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a>. After becoming a priest in 1651 he wrote mainly for the court and the church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-calderon-de-la-barca.jpg" alt="Pedro Calderón de la Barca" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Las_Glorias_Nacionales,_1852_%22D._Pedro_Calder%C3%B3n_de_la_Barca%22_(4013195639).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Calderón was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 17 January 1600 into the minor nobility. His father, Diego Calderón, a mountain hidalgo, held the post of secretary of the Council and Chief Accounting Office of the Treasury and served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> kings Philip II and Philip III. His mother, Ana González de Henao, had family roots in the Spanish Netherlands.</p><p>She died in 1610, when Pedro was ten. He was educated at the Jesuit Colegio Imperial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> with a view to taking orders, but studied law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> instead. He also served as a soldier and became a knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> before he turned to the priesthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His theatrical debut was a history play about King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, first performed on 29 June 1623 at the Royal Alcázar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, during the surprise visit of Charles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, who had come to negotiate a marriage alliance with the Spanish Habsburgs. Born while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega">Lope de Vega</a> was still defining the unwritten rules of Golden Age theatre, Calderón initiated what has been called the second cycle of that theatre. Lope pioneered its forms and genres, and Calderón polished and perfected them. He wrote 120 comedias, 80 autos sacramentales and 20 short comedic pieces called entremeses.</p><p>Because his plays were usually produced at the court of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, he had access to the most modern scenography and worked with Cosme Lotti on complex stage effects, notably in the autos sacramentales. These one-act allegorical plays, performed in the open air during the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> after the procession of the Host, usually treat the miracle of Transubstantiation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Calderón took special care with dramatic structure, using fewer scenes than Lope and fewer metres, and often revisited and reworked his plays long after they were first staged. He made regular use of metafictional devices, having characters joke about the clichés they are expected to follow.</p><p>His cape-and-sword comedies have complicated plots worked out with great precision, and his honour tragedies confront reason with emotion, intellect with instinct and love with vengeance, themes shaped by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> education and his admiration for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and Francisco Suárez. His masterpiece, La vida es sueño, asks whether each person's fate is already written or whether free will can alter the future.</p><p>His reputation fell in the 18th century, and in 1765 a law forbade performance of the autos on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> as sacrilegious and in bad taste. August Wilhelm Schlegel's translations then revived him as a banner figure of German Romanticism. His plays were also translated and performed in English in his lifetime: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys">Samuel Pepys</a> saw free translations from Calderón in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1667, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a> later translated a large part of El mágico prodigioso.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Calderón de la Barca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Spanish Golden Age produced Miguel de Cervantes’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of Lope de Vega and <b>Pedro Calderón de la Barca</b>, the poetry of Luis de Góngora and Francisco de Quevedo, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose Las Meninas dates from 1656, Francisco…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1652" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro Calderón de la Barca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1652">What was here in 1652? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What is life? 'Tis but a madness. What is life? A thing that seems, A mirage that falsely gleams, Phantom joy, delusive rest, Since is life a dream at best, And even dreams themselves are dreams.&#8221;</p><footer>Life Is a Dream (1635), act 2 &#183; tr. Denis Florence MacCarthy, 1873 &#183; spoken by Prince Sigismund &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6363/pg6363-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The king dreams he is a king, And in this delusive way Lives and rules with sovereign sway; All the cheers that round him ring, Born of air, on air take wing.&#8221;</p><footer>Life Is a Dream (1635), act 2 &#183; tr. Denis Florence MacCarthy, 1873 &#183; spoken by Prince Sigismund &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6363/pg6363-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In this world's uncertain gleam, That to live is but to dream: Man dreams what he is, and wakes Only when upon him breaks Death's mysterious morning beam.&#8221;</p><footer>Life Is a Dream (1635), act 2 &#183; tr. Denis Florence MacCarthy, 1873 &#183; spoken by Prince Sigismund &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6363/pg6363-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And the rich man dreams of gold, Gilding cares it scarce conceals, And the poor man dreams he feels Want and misery and cold.&#8221;</p><footer>Life Is a Dream (1635), act 2 &#183; tr. Denis Florence MacCarthy, 1873 &#183; spoken by Prince Sigismund &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6363/pg6363-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
